#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "a1fb7543-873c-5f53-af2b-3f9cf476864f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3533" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\csc x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\csc x &= -4 \\\\\n\\csc x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x < 2\\pi$. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x}< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/dad47ac6e5b2ff506997d004ecfa60e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "ef65c632-0174-5dcf-aacf-40ec3e73e244" :question/math-academy-id "e-3535" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\csc x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\csc x &= -4 \\\\\n\\csc x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi$.\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x}<0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/1ea3d46403714f4a6fd27389cf88708d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2$. To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{3} &= x_{1} + 2π = \\frac{11π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{23π}{6} \\\\\nx_{4} &= x_{2} + 2π = \\frac{7π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{19π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nis outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "5044ded5-04bb-57f8-935c-a4a9264d5055" :question/math-academy-id "e-4262" :question/problem "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} &= 1 \\\\\n\\csc x &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\ntherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{1}{5}) = 0.201357… \\approx 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.201\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/ed103d88ba3ff90d3c2bd12986afbef5.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.201 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.941\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.2, 2.9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff5c81b-6278-5259-963b-8034c690e26c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4263" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand the value\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nis outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nhas no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a49f022-8082-5987-a455-5f916dad4074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113121} {:answer/id #uuid "e797777c-c6d3-5f34-a4cc-49f46fb0f3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113122} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5354dc-42d9-5ca8-ac17-0ac4f3628caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113123} {:answer/id #uuid "569223e5-fb2c-5abe-999c-a6eb31b99451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4π^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113124} {:answer/id #uuid "66786bde-3273-5899-abd5-84c507cec789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5π^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e797777c-c6d3-5f34-a4cc-49f46fb0f3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113122} :answer-field/id #uuid "89e345fe-d943-56e0-89f8-aa8603a36482" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113120}] :question/id #uuid "710ba61e-2523-50bc-83d0-e27883d20d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-29004" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\csc x = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0 \\le x < 2π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c98b33fa-bb8e-571b-a829-9496308b076b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113139} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4a4262-c3a2-5d97-aeca-52663b7a9b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113140} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6d7352-0da6-51f5-a14b-9d5a46223a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113141} {:answer/id #uuid "2fea2216-c805-58c3-afed-38bc8ec0bd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113142} {:answer/id #uuid "4a408bfa-96bc-5dc9-8ac0-f0ae018b9382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4a4262-c3a2-5d97-aeca-52663b7a9b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113140} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb4c78e4-3bca-570b-894a-f4b8d1293b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113138}] :question/id #uuid "05f6d575-2f42-5773-86ea-0000d3bcfcd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-29095" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\csc x = 10\\frac{1}{2}$\n2. $\\csc x = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{1}{2}$\n3. $\\csc x =-1\\frac{1}{2}$"}]}
